+# The Daily Grind
+
+Start the static app with `pnpm start`. Run automated checks with `npm test`.
+
+## WebMCP
+
+`webmcp.js` exposes the page's existing content and navigation through these tools:
+
+| Tool | Input | Result |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `search_seasonal_favorites` | Optional `query` string | Matching visible drinks, descriptions, and detail paths |
+| `list_destinations` | Empty object | Available link labels, paths, and absolute URLs |
+| `navigate_to_destination` | Required `path` from the destination list | Opens that link in the current tab |
+
+Tools use the native `document.modelContext.registerTool()` API, with a fallback to earlier `navigator.modelContext` implementations. A browser with WebMCP support is required for agent discovery. Serve over HTTPS or localhost. Browsers without the API keep the ordinary page experience; no polyfill or external service is installed.
+
+Inputs are validated by the handlers as well as described with JSON Schema. Read tools use the current DOM. Navigation accepts only known links, excludes logout, and does not perform purchases or account changes. The linked routes are the original site's destinations; this static app does not implement their backend functionality.
+
+Registrations are removed on `pagehide` and restored on `pageshow` for back/forward cache navigation. Current implementations use an AbortSignal; earlier implementations use `unregisterTool` when available. Registration failures are logged and roll back registered tools.
+
+The integration follows the [WebMCP draft specification](https://webmachinelearning.github.io/webmcp/) and [Chrome's imperative API guidance](https://github.com/GoogleChrome/modern-web-guidance-src/blob/main/guides/webmcp/agentic-javascript-tools/guide.md).
+
+Tests run in Node with a mocked model context and a minimal DOM adapter reading the actual HTML. They check registration, search, input validation, navigation restrictions, lifecycle, API compatibility, and unsupported browsers. They do not establish native browser interoperability.
